A polynomial-time factorer need not discover a fourth kind of leak. It could produce a residue divisible by exactly one of \(p,q\) on its first informative step. That would give a factor by a gcd and satisfy the repo’s pure-computation theorem exactly. **The strongest necessary conclusion concerns relation methods.** If the algorithm obtains its split from smooth relations, at least one of the priced model’s assumptions must fail. If it obtains its split another way, it can leave all three intact. I would look first for a computation that switches between modular and ordinary integer arithmetic, and second for one that produces a factor directly. [Barrier map](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/BARRIERS.md) · [Completeness audit](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/completeness_audit_results.md:149)

## P1. What the barriers require

Write \(n=\lceil\log_2 N\rceil\). “Cheap” means an ordinary algorithm can leave the stated model with one familiar operation. “Deep” means that making the escape *pay* needs a new mathematical or algorithmic result. The repo describes its proofs as internally audited and awaiting human review. [README](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/README.md)

| Theorem or model | What a polynomial-time algorithm would have to do |
|---|---|
| **Priced relation theorem — deep within the model** | With conditional Dickman smoothness **H**, the stated residual-size profile, and fresh-relation quota **R**, average work per solved target is at least \(L[1/3,(4A/3)^{2/3}]\). Adaptive selection, pooling and batching are already charged. The three hatches are **H**: construct smooth residuals more often than their size and full search history allow. **Profile**: produce enough exceptionally small, independent norms beyond the exception budget, as in the remaining `LP_exc^rel` case with different bases at different embeddings. **R/reuse**: obtain a fruitful dependency from few fresh discoveries, perhaps through a shared descent or independently obtained modular equalities. Charge each target-dependent discovery at first use. The rank-two profile is proved under explicit box and error conditions, leaving small quotas and large boxes as genuine scope limits. [Model theorem](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/lowerbound_results.md) · [Certificate accounting](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/chase_results.md:77) |
| **Sampling, listing and descent — cheap to leave, deep to improve** | Theorem SS says that single-base sampling in polynomial time needs enough candidates of total log-size only \(O((\log n)^2/\log\log n)\). Listing changes the cost to work per usable output. Multiple bases escape SS cheaply. Known variants improve constants. Resultant and area bounds constrain rank two, exact systematic-product identities face Theorem W, and the repo’s descent balances approach \(1/3\). Its “factoring in disguise” verdict describes the direct targets it found. It gives no equivalence between *every* sub-\(1/3\) improvement and polynomial time. Even the unverified Cogentic bound \(L[1/3,105]\), if correct, fits the model. [SS and LS](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/suffmap_results.md:157) · [Cogentic assessment](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/cogentic_results.md) |
| **Pure computation, leaks and amplification — cheap boundaries** | PT charges prediction advantage on independent factors to the probability of a gcd split. PT-m, PT-X, PT-J, NE1 and AC⁰-ε constrain particular residue, exponent, Jacobi or shallow-bit tests. AC⁰-ε concerns the common mod-4 sign on its stated distribution. PRIM further bounds fresh-base chains, sparse sums, certain two-layer gates and bounded-height Jacobi tests. The known mechanisms are group order **M**, ordinary size or proximity **A**, and quadratic genus **Q**. Their unrestricted combinations remain open. Reading integer representatives already gives a computationally complete model. SR1 restricts shared-polynomial factor moves. SR2 shows that an exact public polynomial move of its stated form either preserves the trivial multiplier structure or already factors \(N\). [PT](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/factorbits_results.md:303) · [Leak map](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/leakmap_results.md:442) · [PRIM](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/prim_results.md:24) · [SR1/SR2](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/selfred_results.md:23) |
| **Direct splits, Fourier methods and invariants — deep within each route** | SP/SP-U force large-prime order alignment for sparse torus circuits. Dense programs remain open. Fourier Theorem T requires character contributions that reinforce at a large-order frequency. GR-F charges most such power from componentwise ring operations to existing splits. For native invariants, TRI identifies many encodings of \(p+q\). LOC, LIN, HW, LRS and DFB restrict random-local-data, linear-estimator, fixed-algebra or simulated-trace models. None prices all ways of computing an invariant. [Sparse circuits](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/slpcirc_results.md:7) · [Theorem T](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/periodfind_results.md:120) · [Native models](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/native_results.md) |
| **Search windows and floors — cheap to leave, deep to improve internally** | T1–T3, the multimodulus and coverage bounds, and combined tests CS and conditional CB constrain charged local searches. Prop T maps the ladder’s window lattices with the Howgrave–Graham certificate back into the ordinary univariate Coppersmith lattice. A new source of equations or a different certificate could escape. Exact general quadratic floor sums are NP/#P-hard. The hardness construction misses the balanced top windows. [Coverage models](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/polyimpl_results.md:506) · [Top-block transfer](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/topblock_results.md:15) · [Floor hardness](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/floorhard_results.md:341) |

## P2 and P4. Ranked locations and cheap probes

These are subjective weights **conditional on classical polynomial-time factoring**. I assign a discovery to the operation that makes factor recovery possible, with 15% reserved for mechanisms outside these six. The probes are proposed toy experiments. A positive result on random semiprimes would still need a scaling argument and coverage of hard inputs.

1. **Mixed modular and integer computation — 32%.** Retain a nested power, read its ordinary representative, then use a floor or comparison to choose the next operation. Deterministic PRIM at depth two with a three-term inner expression is already open. Reading the representative goes further. A meaningful signature would be inverse-polynomial advantage on *fresh random masks* of the smaller factor, strong enough to recover it. **Probe:** On held-out moduli and masks \(r\), test the high bit of \((2^N+3^N+r)^N\bmod N\) against the binary inner product of \(r\) and \(p\). Record gcd splits separately and include a planted signal. The repo’s surviving continued-fraction signals decayed like \(N^{-1/4}\), exponentially in input length. [PRIM frontier](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/prim_results.md:24) · [Entropy experiments](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/entropy_results.md)

2. **Direct splitting in a richer group — 18%.** Seek a short matrix word that becomes the identity modulo exactly one hidden prime with inverse-polynomial probability. PT permits this. Single-scalar annihilation and sparse torus bounds explain why simply increasing the dimension buys little. **Probe:** Iterate a fixed commutator word on random determinant-one \(2\times2\) matrices. Measure identity rates prime by prime, then verify the predicted proper gcds on blind products, including primes where \(p-1\) and \(p+1\) have large prime factors. Persistent splits there would distinguish the candidate from ordinary smooth-order success. [Order-annihilation barrier](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/nonsmooth_results.md:268)

3. **Native evaluation of an invariant — 14%.** A fast direct computation of \(\sigma(N)\) or a suitable curve point count has an established recovery step. The single non-CM coefficient \(A=a_f(N)\) has **two** missing steps: native evaluation and factor recovery from \((N,A)\). DFB leaves open a coupling between the true coefficient and arithmetic on the same curve. **Probe:** Supply the true \(A\) for curve 37a1 in the test harness. Compute \([N+1-A](0,0)\) on the curve modulo \(N\) and measure proper gcds of its projective denominator against shuffled-\(A\) controls. This cheaply tests the recovery step before investing in evaluation. [Inventory of 55 invariants](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/native_results.md) · [Single-coefficient model](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/cuspcoef_results.md:24)

4. **Lattices supplied with genuinely new information — 10%.** For balanced factors, roughly \(n/4\) contiguous bits of \(p\) suffice. Recovering \(p\) from fewer supplied bits would improve partial-key recovery. A factorer must also generate its hints from \(N\), or cover the possibilities in polynomial work. **Probe:** Generate several public nested-power residues and test whether they are simultaneously unusually close to multiples of the *same* hidden prime. Compare with matched random residues, then give only the public samples to an approximate-common-divisor decoder. Blind recovery would be a stronger signature than another short vector that vanishes on the whole known curve. [Top blocks](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/topblock_results.md) · [Hint-generation costs](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/equivtarget_results.md)

5. **Constructive relations beyond the Johnson decoding radius — 7%.** The target is polynomial *total* work for enough independent, fruitful relations. A constructor for one short interval near \(kN\) yields only the heuristic \(L[1/3,1.2114]\). **Probe:** On blinded 64–96-bit quadratic CRT instances, compare an adaptive decoder with direct sieving for one CPU-hour. Count branch selection, failed candidates and work per independent row. A growing advantage after matching sizes and known divisors would be evidence against H. The repo’s guidance experiments found none. [Decoder proposal](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/equivtarget_results.md) · [Guidance controls](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/guide_results.md)

6. **Fast modular factorial — 4%.** Uniformly compute \(k!\bmod N\) for binary \(k\le\sqrt N\). Binary search for the first \(k\) with nonunit gcd then gives the least prime factor. **Probe:** Test a proposed fixed-width doubling recurrence at \(2k\) and \(2k+1\) against exact products on fresh composite moduli, charging every precomputed coefficient. The signature is correct evaluation through the first gcd transition with work scaling in \(\log k\). The multiplicity construction examined by the repo first sees the prime at order about \(p\), making fast evaluation the entire issue. [Factorial route](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/slpcirc_results.md) · [Multiplicity test](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/multcopp_results.md)

## P3. Consistency checks

- **Consequences one can verify.** Multiply the returned primes and check primality. A bounded-error factorer can therefore be retried in expected polynomial time. For \(N=pq\), verify \(p+q=N+1-\phi(N)=\sigma(N)-N-1\). Factoring also gives multiplicative orders with further calls on \(p-1,q-1\), and fixed-form coefficients from prime-level computations and multiplicativity. Bach–Charles use **both** \(a_f(N)\) and \(a_f(N^2)\) to factor a density-one set of RSA moduli. [Implication catalogue](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/polyimpl_results.md:150) · [Bach–Charles]https://arxiv.org/abs/0708.1192)
- **Discrete logarithms are a route-specific check.** No known reduction gives prime-field DL from factoring alone. Composite DL is equivalent under randomized reductions to factoring plus prime-field DL. Zech addition is DL-equivalent in its own field. The repo’s unrestricted-height navigator has a factoring-plus-DL equivalence under its generation assumptions. Its modulus-generic coset lister transfers under index-calculus rank assumptions, and its strong Fourier observable gives DL on the covered primes. Each is an extra prediction of that mechanism. [Bach]https://www2.eecs.berkeley.edu/Pubs/TechRpts/1984/5973.html) · [Navigation](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/navigate_results.md:35) · [Fourier reduction](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/prepread_results.md:40)
- **Conjectural exclusions.** A short constant-free, division-free integer circuit for the Pochhammer polynomial would refute the Shub–Smale \(\tau\)-conjecture through its many integer roots. Such circuits give nonuniform factoring unless uniformly constructible. Bürgisser’s implication runs from easy permanent to short factorial circuits, so a suitable factorial-circuit **lower bound** proves a permanent lower bound. Fast modular factorials or fast factoring alone imply neither result. Likewise, a deterministic general floor-sum oracle would give FP = #P and P = NP. These are reasons to discount those specific routes. [Bürgisser]https://eccc.weizmann.ac.il/report/2006/113/download/) · [Floor-sum reduction](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/floorhard_results.md:341)
- **Hardcore bits.** Fast factoring makes every factor bit computable, consistently with hardcore-bit theorems. Goldreich–Levin gives recovery from a predictor with sufficient advantage over fresh random masks. RSA message-bit reductions have their own movable secret. A persistent bias on one fixed bit of \(p\) has no known general amplification. That is why the first probe asks for a mask and the second asks for an actual gcd. [Goldreich–Levin](https://www.cs.bu.edu/fac/lnd/pdf/hard.pdf) · [Factor-bit distinctions](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/factorbits_results.md:207)